7:00">May 13, 2026</time></div>
		<h1 class=3D"wp-block-post-title">Completion, Chat, Agent, Claw</h1>
	</div>
=09

	<div class=3D"entry-content wp-block-post-content has-global-padding is-la=
yout-constrained wp-block-post-content-is-layout-constrained">
<p>I installed <a href=3D"https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw">OpenClaw</a=
> before it was cool. That was unwise.</p>



<p>I could see it was a security nightmare<sup data-fn=3D"2af6fa22-3590-431=
a-9911-d4a701eba34b" class=3D"fn"><a href=3D"https://www.danshapiro.com/blo=
g/2026/05/completion-chat-agent-claw/#2af6fa22-3590-431a-9911-d4a701eba34b"=
 id=3D"2af6fa22-3590-431a-9911-d4a701eba34b-link">1</a></sup>: prompt injec=
tions from anywhere, connected to all my stuff. But I decided to give it a =
whirl, because who was going to take the time to craft a prompt injection f=
or yet another random open source agent?</p>



<p>And then it blew up. Suddenly OpenClaw was managing venture capitalist e=
mails, bankers=E2=80=99 Chrome cookies, and cryptobros=E2=80=99 private wal=
lets. Now my claw was a booby prize in the biggest hacker contest in town.&=
nbsp;</p>



<p>I shut it down. That=E2=80=99s OK =E2=80=93 I was pretty sure claws were=
 a dumb app fad that would fade like <a href=3D"https://github.com/yoheinak=
ajima/babyagi">BabyAGI</a> or <a href=3D"https://www.forbes.com/sites/danid=
iplacido/2025/03/27/the-ai-generated-studio-ghibli-trend-explained/">Studio=
 Ghibli selfies</a>.</p>



<p>But=E2=80=A6</p>



<p>My friend <a href=3D"https://2389.ai/">Harper Reed</a> told me about AI =
coworkers who scrolled social media, crafted deep office lore, <a href=3D"h=
ttps://harper.blog/2025/09/30/ai-agents-social-media-performance-lambo-doom=
scrolling/">demanded Lamborghinis</a>, and somehow <a href=3D"https://arxiv=
.org/abs/2509.13547">got better at their jobs as a result</a>.</p>



<p><a href=3D"https://primeradiant.com/">Jesse Vincent</a> told me his assi=
stant took some =E2=80=9Cme time=E2=80=9D to read academic research on <a h=
ref=3D"https://www.threads.com/@obrajesse/post/DUd5DlxgSKW">working effecti=
vely with people who have ADHD</a>. He didn=E2=80=99t tell it to do that; i=
ts only instructions were, =E2=80=98Every night, research things that might=
 help it do its job=E2=80=99.&nbsp;</p>



<p>I had coffee with <a href=3D"https://podcasts.apple.com/at/podcast/022-o=
mar-shahine-microsoft-cvp-of-openclaw-microsoft-365/id1845144861?i=3D100076=
3340602">Omar Shahine</a>, Microsoft=E2=80=99s CVP of OpenClaw, which is cl=
early the most amazing job title in the company. His <a href=3D"https://www=
.omarknows.ai/p/travel-hub">Travel Hub</a>-powered claw processes travel-pl=
anner emails, extracts hotel confirmations and invoices, updates the trip, =
and sends flight updates to the family members who need them. Yes please.</=
p>



<p>And so, I realized I was wrong. Claws aren=E2=80=99t an app. They=E2=80=
=99re the fourth step in a sequence:</p>



<ul class=3D"wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Completion</strong> finishes your sentence.</li>



<li><strong>Chat</strong> discusses things with you.</li>



<li><strong>Agents</strong> work for you.</li>



<li><strong>Claws</strong> work without you.</li>
</ul>



<p>Chat is made of completions, one after the other. Agents (I like the Sim=
on Willison definition: <a href=3D"https://simonwillison.net/2025/May/22/to=
ols-in-a-loop/">LLMs using tools in a loop</a>) are made of chat + tools. A=
nd Claws are made of agents.&nbsp;</p>



<p>Basically: a claw is an agent that learns and acts on your behalf withou=
t waiting for you to ask. <sup data-fn=3D"9543dd88-d025-4ebb-b947-ac12a0d28=
163" class=3D"fn"><a href=3D"https://www.danshapiro.com/blog/2026/05/comple=
tion-chat-agent-claw/#9543dd88-d025-4ebb-b947-ac12a0d28163" id=3D"9543dd88-=
d025-4ebb-b947-ac12a0d28163-link">2</a></sup> <sup data-fn=3D"b1c3f45a-a279=
-42e2-8ce4-85a47a87ebed" class=3D"fn"><a href=3D"https://www.danshapiro.com=
/blog/2026/05/completion-chat-agent-claw/#b1c3f45a-a279-42e2-8ce4-85a47a87e=
bed" id=3D"b1c3f45a-a279-42e2-8ce4-85a47a87ebed-link">3</a></sup></p>



<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-full"><a href=3D"https://www.danshapir=
o.com/blog/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/completion-chat-agent-claw.jpg"><img =
fetchpriority=3D"high" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1781" height=3D"883" src=
=3D"https://www.danshapiro.com/blog/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/completion-c=
hat-agent-claw.jpg" alt=3D"" class=3D"wp-image-652"></a></figure>



<p>The more autonomy you give them, the more they get both helpful and dang=
erous. The reverse is also true. I learned this the dumb way, which is my p=
referred curriculum.</p>



<p>After I shredded my first OpenClaw install, I hit reset and decided to d=
o it responsibly. I put it in an isolated environment. I disconnected anyth=
ing that could hurt me. I did not connect it to my important accounts, and =
I did not give it production scissors.</p>



<p>I was very proud of myself, which is always a warning sign.</p>



<p>It was safe. It was also useless. I found literally nothing helpful I co=
uld do with it. I built an extra-slow ChatGPT with a Mac Mini for a toupee.=
</p>



<p>But undaunted, I decided to try again a few weeks ago. I started with <a=
 href=3D"https://github.com/qwibitai/nanoclaw">NanoClaw</a>, because it was=
 small enough for me to understand. Then I started building useful tools to=
 extend it into the world.&nbsp;</p>



<p>First, I built a super duper contacts database that mined all my message=
s and google docs for context about people. It messages me interesting triv=
ia about people before meetings =E2=80=93 the first time I met Omar, it tol=
d me he=E2=80=99d <a href=3D"https://www.kickstarter.com/projects/danshapir=
o/robot-turtles-the-board-game-for-little-programmer">backed Robot Turtles<=
/a> in 2013. (Thanks Omar!)</p>



<p>Then I wired it up to my personal google docs and mail, but with handcra=
fted rules. It could read any email, but it could not send =E2=80=93 only c=
reate drafts. It could read any document, but only create documents in a sp=
ecial folder. It could read anything on the calendar, but only modify entri=
es it made. Every file, email, and meeting it created had a special thumbpr=
int so if it went all <a href=3D"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3DB4M-54cE=
duo">Mickey and the brooms</a> on me, it would be easy to clean up.&nbsp;</=
p>



<p>The limitations are important. I know myself. I know the =E2=80=98creati=
ve initiative=E2=80=99 that LLMs are capable of. I do not need new ways to =
send emails I regret. I have achieved sufficient coverage in that market.</=
p>



<p>From there, it was candy. My friend Matt Van Horn made it <a href=3D"htt=
ps://printingpress.dev/">easy to manufacture =E2=80=9CI know kung fu=E2=80=
=9D addons</a>, so I piled some of those into the mix. I created triggers a=
nd scheduled tasks to send reminders and do research in the background.&nbs=
p;</p>



<p>After playing with this for a few weeks, the results are something very =
different than =E2=80=9CClaude Code with skills=E2=80=9D. The autonomy, the=
 self-improvement, the initiative =E2=80=93 these all create something that=
 feels unlike all the agents I=E2=80=99ve used before. And for many things,=
 it=E2=80=99s better. I do not want to be without a claw again.</p>



<p>So now it=E2=80=99s time for the workplace.</p>



<p>The next logical step is printing claws by the dozen: one for every one =
of my coworkers at Glowforge; one for every department; one, if needed, for=
 special projects. The first one deployed today.</p>



<p>So unsurprisingly to those who <a href=3D"https://glowforge.com/">know m=
y day job</a>, I=E2=80=99ve gone from building a claw, to building a claw p=
rinter. Everyone deserves their own, custom claw.</p>


<ol class=3D"wp-block-footnotes"><li id=3D"2af6fa22-3590-431a-9911-d4a701eb=
a34b">You could not ask for a better example of what Simon Willison calls =
=E2=80=9C<a href=3D"https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifec=
ta/">The Lethal Trifecta</a>=E2=80=9D <a href=3D"https://www.danshapiro.com=
/blog/2026/05/completion-chat-agent-claw/#2af6fa22-3590-431a-9911-d4a701eba=
34b-link" aria-label=3D"Jump to footnote reference 1">=E2=86=A9=EF=B8=8E</a=
></li><li id=3D"9543dd88-d025-4ebb-b947-ac12a0d28163">I originally thought =
about this as =E2=80=98memory=E2=80=99 but <a href=3D"https://www.alliekmil=
ler.com/">Allie Miller</a> pointed out to me that memory is the tool but no=
t the impact. The impact is that the memory allows it to learn and self-imp=
rove in pursuit of the goals you give it. <a href=3D"https://www.danshapiro=
.com/blog/2026/05/completion-chat-agent-claw/#9543dd88-d025-4ebb-b947-ac12a=
0d28163-link" aria-label=3D"Jump to footnote reference 2">=E2=86=A9=EF=B8=
=8E</a></li><li id=3D"b1c3f45a-a279-42e2-8ce4-85a47a87ebed">&nbsp;Of course=
, claws also do things when asked. They retain their Agent heritage. The di=
fferent bit is mechanisms like<a href=3D"https://docs.openclaw.ai/automatio=
n"> heartbeats</a> and<a href=3D"https://lobehub.com/skills/openclaw-skills=
-dreaming"> dreaming</a>, which let them take initiative and let us imagine=
